{"name":"Foil","hex":"#c0c3c4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"foil","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A very thin sheet of metal.","A very thin sheet of plastic."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover or wrap with foil.","Clipping of hydrofoil."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/foil"}],"entries":[{"title":"Foil (metal)","blocks":[{"text":"A foil is a very thin sheet of metal, typically made by hammering or rolling. Foils are most easily made with malleable metal, such as aluminium, copper, tin, and gold. Foils usually bend under their own weight and can be torn easily. For example, aluminium foil is usually about 1⁄1000 inch (0.025 mm), whereas gold can be made into foil only a few atoms thick, called gold leaf. Extremely thin foil is called metal leaf. Leaf tears very easily and must be picked up with special brushes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Aluminio.jpg/330px-Aluminio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foil_(metal)","wikidata":"Q251473"},{"title":"Metal leaf","blocks":[{"text":"A metal leaf, also called composition leaf or schlagmetal, is a thin foil used for gilding and other forms of decoration. Metal leaves can come in many different shades, due to the composition of the metal within the metal leaf. Examples of this variation of shades in metal leaves can be found in Ancient Egyptian gold leaves, as the silver content within the gold leaves could make them appear bright yellow or paler shades of yellow. Some metal leaves may look like gold leaf but do not contain any real gold. This type of metal leaf is often referred to as imitation leaf."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Kanazawa_Gold_Factory.jpg/330px-Kanazawa_Gold_Factory.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metal_leaf","wikidata":"Q11074667"},{"title":"Foil (fencing)","blocks":[{"text":"A foil is one of the three weapons used in the sport of fencing. It is a flexible weapon of total length 110 cm (43 in) or under, rectangular in cross section, weighing under 500 g (18 oz), with a blunt tip. As with the épée, points are only scored by making contact with the tip. The foil is the most commonly used weapon in fencing."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Final_2013_Fencing_WCH_FMS-IN_t200907.jpg/330px-Final_2013_Fencing_WCH_FMS-IN_t200907.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foil_(fencing)","wikidata":"Q471447"},{"title":"Foil (narrative)","blocks":[{"text":"In any narrative, a foil is a character who contrasts with another character, typically, a character who contrasts with the protagonist, in order to better highlight or differentiate certain qualities of the protagonist. A foil to the protagonist may also be the antagonist of the plot."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Don_Quixote_and_Sancho_Panza_by_Gustave_Dore_%2892687408%29.jpg/330px-Don_Quixote_and_Sancho_Panza_by_Gustave_Dore_%2892687408%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foil_(narrative)","wikidata":"Q5563727"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/ItaloSantelli1900Olympics.jpg/330px-ItaloSantelli1900Olympics.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/ItaloSantelli1900Olympics.jpg/330px-ItaloSantelli1900Olympics.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" Italo Santelli (left) and Jean-Baptiste Mimiague exhibiting techniques of foil fencing at the 1900 Olympics","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AItaloSantelli1900Olympics.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Foil-2004-A.jpg/500px-Foil-2004-A.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Foil-2004-A.jpg/500px-Foil-2004-A.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A foil fitted with an Italian grip. While still in use with a small number of classical fencers, all competitive sport fencers have abandoned the Italian grip in favor of variations of the pistol grip, with the French grip used rarely. The last Olympic medal won using an Italian grip was Carlo Montano's silver in 1976.[10] The French grip is easier to learn, but the pistol grip gives a wider range of handling. As of March 2019, the Italian grip remains legal for use in modern competition.[11]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFoil-2004-A.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Anatomyoffoil.jpg/500px-Anatomyoffoil.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Anatomyoffoil.jpg/1280px-Anatomyoffoil.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Anatomyoffoil.jpg/500px-Anatomyoffoil.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" Parts of a foil. Exploded view of a modern fencing foil, with a Visconti grip and a bayonet-style body cord plug[citation needed]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAnatomyoffoil.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Pariser.jpg/500px-Pariser.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Pariser.jpg/1280px-Pariser.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src"